    "claim": "Popper's preference for better-corroborated theories is defensible on his own terms not as an inductive inference of truth, but as a non-inductive methodological rule grounded in the logic of falsification: we prefer the theory that has survived severe tests because it has higher empirical content and has not yet been falsified, making it the best available target for future criticism. This preference does not smuggle in induction because it makes no claim about the future probability of the theory being true; it only asserts that, relative to our current critical discussion, this theory is the most worthy of further testing. The cost of accepting this view is the abandonment of the hope that science converges toward probable truth or even definable verisimilitude (given the Miller-Tichý refutation), leaving us with a purely procedural rationality where 'progress' is defined solely by the elimination of error, not the accumulation of confirmed knowledge.",
